# LPA Web UI tests (lean suite)

Track phased review in this workspace: [lpa-web-ui-code-review.md](../../lpa-web-ui-code-review.md) (code **and** `*.spec.ts` per phase).

## Philosophy

- **Test what can hurt users or data**, not every line of UI. A lean suite is easier to maintain and faster to run.
- **One spec per behavior**, not per component method. If two `it()` blocks assert the same user-visible rule, merge or delete one.
- **Prefer pure helpers** (`*.ts` next to features) for tricky logic (validation, coordinate math, keyboard maps). One focused spec file beats many shallow component tests.
- **Storybook is not a test.** Stories help eyes; specs guard regressions that matter.
- **Removing tests is allowed** when reviewing shows they only duplicate implementation detail, assert framework behavior, or break whenever copy/CSS changes without catching real bugs.

## Write a spec when failure would likely mean…

| Risk | Examples |
|------|-----------|
| **Data loss or corruption** | Saving labels, project config patches, wrong RPC payload shape |
| **Silent wrong science** | Viewer frame/time jumps, likelihood threshold hiding points, multiview mixing models |
| **Blocked workflow** | Cannot save, cannot open label file, dialog never closes after success |
| **Security / paths** | Path traversal in user-entered dirs, registering wrong project root |
| **Non-obvious rules** | Skeleton validity catalog, undirected pair deduping, bones not crossing views/models |

## Usually skip (or keep minimal)

- “Component creates” / `should create` with no behavior assertion.
- Testing that a child component was called when the parent only forwards an `@Input` (test the child or the integration once).
- Exact CSS class lists or pixel-perfect layout unless the class **is** the behavior (e.g. error state `text-error`).
- Duplicating the same rule in a parent spec and a child spec—**one owner** per rule (see feature plans).
- Exhaustive permutations of valid form input when one happy path + one rejection path suffices.

## Per-spec scrutiny (during code review)

For each `it()` / `describe()` in the phase’s `*.spec.ts` files, ask:

1. **If this failed in CI, would we fix a real bug or just update the test?** If mostly the latter, remove or narrow the assertion.
2. **Is the behavior already covered elsewhere?** Drop the duplicate; note the surviving spec in CHANGELOG/BUGS/feature plan.
3. **Does it mock at the right layer?** Mock `RpcService` / `ProjectInfoService` / `SessionService`; do not integration-test the backend from Karma.
4. **Does it assert user-observable outcomes?** Clicks, keyboard, `aria-label`, emitted outputs, DOM text—not private methods.

Record decisions in the review tracker: **keep** · **trim** (fewer cases) · **remove** · **add** (gap) · **merge** (into helper spec).

## When you change behavior

- Update or add **one** spec that names the regression you fear—not a spec per file touched.
- In [CHANGELOG.md](../../lightning-pose-app/CHANGELOG.md), [BUGS.md](../../BUGS.md), or `feature_plans/*.md`, cite that spec and **what failure mode** it guards—not “has unit tests”.

## Commands

```bash
cd lightning-pose-app/web_ui && npx ng lint
npx ng test --no-watch --browsers=ChromeHeadless --include=<path/to/spec.ts>
```

Run only specs for code you changed unless the change is shared (`components/`, routing, global styles).
